  <script type="application/ld+json">
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    "mainEntity": [
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Do lawyers need to remove PDF metadata before sharing documents?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Yes. PDF metadata can expose the Author name, Manager name, internal Keywords from document management systems, edit timestamps, and software used — none of which are relevant to the recipient and some of which may be privileged. Under Model Rules of Professional Conduct Rule 1.6 (confidentiality) and ABA Formal Opinion 11-460, lawyers have an obligation to use reasonable care to avoid inadvertent disclosure of client information, which includes metadata in shared documents. Before sharing any PDF externally, lawyers should strip metadata using a dedicated tool that removes both the Info dictionary and XMP stream."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What PDF metadata should HR teams remove before sending offer letters?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "HR teams should remove the Author field (contains the HR professional's name — personal data under GDPR), Manager field (may contain the HR manager's name), Keywords (may contain internal job codes, salary bands, or candidate classifications from ATS systems), and modification timestamps (reveal when the offer letter was last edited, which can indicate negotiation activity). All of these fields should be stripped from both the PDF Info dictionary and the XMP stream before the document is sent to the candidate."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Can PDF metadata expose a journalist's source?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "PDF metadata can expose a journalist's identity and working patterns but not usually the source directly. The Author field contains the journalist's name, timestamps reveal when documents were accessed or modified, and the Creator field identifies the software used. Indirectly, if a journalist edits a document shared by a source and the modified version retains metadata, the modification timestamp may help narrow down when the journalist and source were in contact. For source protection, journalists should use SecureDrop for receiving documents and strip all metadata before sharing or publishing processed versions."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Does PDF metadata in a freelance proposal reveal confidential information?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "PDF metadata in a freelance proposal can reveal: the freelancer's full name (Author field), their manager's name if using a corporate account (Manager field), internal pricing notes in the Keywords field, how many times the proposal was revised (RevisionNumber — revealing whether it was bespoke or adapted from a template), when it was last modified (revealing whether it was prepared at the last minute), and which software was used (Creator — which may contradict a premium positioning if the proposal was made in a free tool)."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Is sharing PDF metadata with a client a GDPR violation?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Sharing a PDF containing the Author field — which contains an employee's full name — with an external party constitutes sharing personal data under GDPR Article 4(1). If that personal data serves no purpose for the recipient, sharing it may violate the data minimisation principle under Article 5(1)(c). This applies to any organisation subject to GDPR or UK GDPR sharing documents externally. The practical fix is to strip the Author and Manager fields before sharing — a process that takes under 30 seconds with a dedicated tool."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What is the fastest way to remove PDF metadata before sending a professional document?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "The fastest method that removes both the Info dictionary and XMP stream is FileIntel's PDF Metadata Remover at fileintel.me/pdf-metadata-remover — drop the PDF in, click Strip Metadata, download the clean file in under 10 seconds. Nothing is uploaded. For command line users: exiftool -all= document.pdf followed by exiftool -XMP:all document.pdf to verify the XMP stream was cleared."
        }
      }
    ]
  }
  </script>

