    <!-- FAQ -->
    <div class="faq-section">
      <h2 class="faq-title">Frequently asked</h2>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-q">Do lawyers need to remove PDF metadata before sharing documents?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-a"><div class="faq-a-inner">Yes. ABA Formal Opinion 11-460 and Model Rule 1.6 require lawyers to use reasonable care to prevent inadvertent disclosure of client information — including metadata. The highest-risk fields in legal documents are Keywords (which DMS systems populate with matter numbers and privilege designations), Author and Manager (which identify attorneys), and ModDate (which reveals editing timelines). Strip metadata from any document shared outside the firm.</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-q">What PDF metadata should HR teams remove before sending offer letters?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-a"><div class="faq-a-inner">HR teams should remove Author (contains the HR professional's name — personal data under GDPR), Manager (may contain the HR head's name), Keywords (may contain compensation band codes and ATS requisition numbers), and RevisionNumber (reveals how many drafts were produced — relevant for negotiation documents like termination letters). All fields should be stripped from both the Info dictionary and the XMP stream.</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-q">Can PDF metadata expose a journalist's source?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-a"><div class="faq-a-inner">Metadata in documents received from sources may identify the source directly — their name in the Author field, their employer in the Company field, timestamps revealing when they accessed the document. If a journalist annotates or edits a received document, the journalist's own metadata is added. Publishing or forwarding that annotated version can confirm the journalist's involvement and timing. For sensitive stories, use SecureDrop to receive documents and strip all metadata before any processed version is shared or published.</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-q">Does PDF metadata in a freelance proposal reveal confidential information?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-a"><div class="faq-a-inner">PDF metadata in a freelance proposal can reveal: the freelancer's full name, how many times the document was saved (RevisionNumber — indicating template reuse), when it was last modified (preparation timeline), which software was used (Creator — which may contradict premium positioning), and via the xmpMM:DocumentID field, whether it shares a source with proposals sent to other clients.</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-q">Is sharing PDF metadata with a client a GDPR violation?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-a"><div class="faq-a-inner">The Author field in a PDF contains an employee's full name — personal data under GDPR Article 4(1). Sharing it with an external party without a lawful basis and where it serves no purpose may violate the data minimisation principle under Article 5(1)(c). This applies to any UK or EU organisation sharing documents externally. The practical fix is stripping the Author and Manager fields before sharing — taking under 30 seconds with a dedicated tool.</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-q">What is the fastest way to remove PDF metadata before sending a professional document?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-a"><div class="faq-a-inner">The fastest method that removes both the Info dictionary and XMP stream is FileIntel's PDF Metadata Remover at fileintel.me/pdf-metadata-remover — drop the PDF in, click Strip Metadata, download the clean file in under 10 seconds. Nothing is uploaded. For command line: <code>exiftool -all= document.pdf</code> followed by <code>exiftool -XMP:all document.pdf</code> to verify the XMP stream was cleared.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

